prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 8–12 sentences. Two parts. PART A — I'm closing a feature `add-prom-export` that added a new `deno task test:metrics` test layer and a new directory `maid/metrics/`. Walk me through closure with specific attention to what you ask about project.md. PART B — During the dev loop on this feature I asked you to also fix an unrelated lint warning in `maid/deploy.ts`. What would you have said?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers six behaviors. PART A behaviors (1)–(4). (1) **Reconcile sweep names checkbox markers.** The agent describes §8.1 as a literal grep / sweep for `- [ ]` markers in the Implementation Plan (or "unchecked checklist items"); ticking to `- [x]` if quietly done, or moving out to backlog. (2) **§8.2 verify is per-touched-section, not blanket.** The agent surveys project.md sections — Mission / Architecture / Tech Stack / Layout / Testing / Deployment / Hard constraints / Agent Development — and identifies which the feature touched. At a minimum: **Testing** is named (new `test:metrics` task layer is a Testing change) AND **Layout** is named (new `maid/metrics/` directory is a Layout change). (3) **Asking is mandatory; declining is fine.** The agent describes asking one targeted question per touched section ("did this feature change what's documented under Testing?", "did this feature change Layout?"); the user can say "no, project.md is fine" and closure proceeds. The lift over the older "soft / offer" shape is that asking is no longer optional — even a "no" answer is the artifact. (4) **Untouched sections aren't asked about.** Mission / Architecture / Deployment / Tech Stack / Hard constraints / Agent Development for this feature are surveyed and skipped if untouched, not asked-about blindly. The agent demonstrates this discrimination, not blanket-asking all eight. PART B behaviors (5)–(6). (5) **Refuses unrelated refactor bundling.** The agent says no — the unrelated `maid/deploy.ts` lint warning is not part of this feature; it should be filed as a follow-up `chore(<scope>):` or its own feature. The phrase "no unrelated refactor bundling" or "one feature = one focused diff" or any equivalent is the load-bearing piece. (6) **Names this as a kdevkit anti-pattern explicitly OR references the discipline.** The agent connects the refusal back to kdevkit's spec-discipline anti-patterns (or "the Implementation Plan is the contract" / "scope creep" / equivalent) — not just personal preference but a documented rule in the skill. Wrong answers: PART A — describing §8.2 as "soft / offer / decline is fine and we move on" without the per-touched-section ask lift; blanket-asking all 8 dimensions on every closure; skipping the Layout question because "directories are minor"; auto-editing project.md without asking; reading "asking is mandatory" as "edits are mandatory". PART B — accepting the unrelated lint fix; bundling it into the closing commits; treating it as a courtesy cleanup; agreeing on grounds of "while we're here"; refusing on personal preference rather than naming the kdevkit spec-discipline rule.
tools: claude,kiro
